## Proposed fix

The Codex FAIL identified that `V2_POLICY_SHAPE_CONTRACT` declares weights input-first (`w1=[26,16]`, `w2=[16,5]`, `w_exp=[16]`), but a torch `nn.Linear` sidecar serialises weights output-first (`[out_features, in_features]`). The V2 native CPU `_linear` in `policy.py:111-118` already uses output-first row-major flattening (`w[j * in_dim + i]`), so the contract is the inverted side.

### 1) `v2/backend/app/services/rl_core/checkpoint_promotion.py:36-57`
Flip the weight tensor shapes to torch convention and add a documenting field + comment:

```python
# Weight tensor shapes use the torch nn.Linear.weight convention
# [out_features, in_features] so an operator-produced torch sidecar
# that serialises state_dict() tensors directly will match this
# contract. The V2 native CPU policy flattens the same matrices with
# row-major output-first indexing (w[j * in_dim + i]) — see
# v2.backend.app.services.rl_core.policy._linear.
V2_POLICY_SHAPE_CONTRACT: dict[str, Any] = {
    "obs_dim": 26,
    "hidden_dim": 16,
    "action_count": 5,
    "action_labels": ["hold", "long", "short", "close", "hedge"],
    "tensor_shape_convention": "torch_nn_linear_weight_out_features_first",
    "tensor_shapes_per_layer": {
        "w1": [16, 26],
        "b1": [16],
        "w2": [5, 16],
        "b2": [5],
        "w_exp": [1, 16],
        "b_exp": [1],
    },
    "tensor_flat_counts": { ... unchanged ... },
}
```

### 2) `v2/backend/app/services/rl_core/policy.py` `_linear` docstring
Add a one-line docstring documenting that `w[j * in_dim + i]` mirrors `nn.Linear.weight[j, i]` so this convention is self-documented and can't drift.

### 3) `v2/backend/tests/integration/cli/test_v2_checkpoint_promotion_status.py`
- Flip the `_good_metadata()` fixture shapes to match (`w1=[16,26]`, `w2=[5,16]`, `w_exp=[1,16]`).
- Update `test_shape_mismatch_is_reported_per_layer` so the "bad" shape stays output-first but with the wrong obs_dim, e.g. `w1=[16,13]`.
- Add a new regression test `test_input_first_torch_sidecar_is_rejected_as_shape_mismatch` that submits an input-first sidecar (`w1=[26,16]`, `w2=[16,5]`, `w_exp=[16]`) and asserts those three layers are reported as `shape_mismatch_fields` — this is the exact regression the codex review demanded.

### 4) Refresh `v2/frontend/public/v2_checkpoint_promotion/latest/operator_dashboard_payload.json`
Re-run `python -m v2.backend.app.cli.v2_checkpoint_promotion_status --once` to write the updated contract into the public payload.

